---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const webCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · HTML — the structure', note: 'Elements with meaning: <h1> a heading, <p> a paragraph, <img> an image. The browser builds a tree (the DOM) from it. Semantic HTML — choosing tags for meaning — is what makes pages accessible and findable.' },
    { math: '2 · CSS — the appearance', note: 'Rules that select elements and style them: colours, layout, fonts. The box model (content, padding, border, margin) and flexbox/grid cover most of layout work.' },
    { math: '3 · JavaScript — the behaviour', note: 'The only language browsers execute natively. It reacts to clicks, fetches data, and rewrites the page live — single-threaded and event-driven, which shapes everything about how web code is written.' },
    { math: '4 · The round trip', note: 'Browser requests HTML → builds DOM → applies CSS → runs JS → fetches more data (fetch()) → updates the DOM. Modern frameworks (React, Vue) just automate that last loop.' },
  ],
};

const webQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: "A stylesheet has: p { color: blue } and .note { color: green }. A paragraph is <p class='note'>. What colour is it, and why?",
      choices: [
        "Blue — element rules beat class rules",
        "Green — a class selector (0,0,1,0) outweighs an element selector (0,0,0,1) in the cascade",
        "It depends on which appears first in the file",
        "Neither — conflicting rules cancel out",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "The cascade resolves conflicts by specificity: id (0,1,0,0) beats class (0,0,1,0) beats element (0,0,0,1); source order only breaks ties. Debugging 'why is my style ignored' is usually a specificity audit.",
    },
    {
      q: "A site renders comments by inserting user text directly into HTML. A user types <script>steal()</script> as a comment. What happened?",
      choices: [
        "Nothing — browsers ignore script tags in text",
        "XSS (cross-site scripting): the text executes as code in every visitor's browser — the fix is escaping user content",
        "A syntax error, caught by the HTML validator",
        "A SQL injection attack",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "This is the web's classic injection lesson: any boundary between data and code that the HTML parser does not know about becomes an attack surface. Escaping (rendering < as &lt;) closes it — security and correctness are the same work here.",
    },
    {
      q: "JavaScript is single-threaded, yet a page can fetch data for 2 seconds and stay responsive. How?",
      choices: [
        "The browser runs JS on multiple cores automatically",
        "The fetch is handed to the browser; the event loop runs other tasks and resumes your callback when the result arrives",
        "The page pauses and resumes invisibly",
        "fetch blocks the thread but users do not notice",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "Blocking work (network, timers) is delegated to the browser; your code continues via callbacks/promises when the event loop delivers the result. Long synchronous loops, not fetches, are what freeze pages.",
    },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/web-development-html-css-js">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The web runs on three languages with a clean division of labour: <strong>HTML is the structure, CSS
    the appearance, JavaScript the behaviour</strong> — the very stack this site is built on. The browser
    is the most widely deployed runtime in history (no install, every device), and the division of labour
    is what makes the platform durable: structure that works without the other two, presentation that can
    be restyled wholesale, behaviour layered on top. This lesson builds the trio properly — the pipeline
    from file to pixels, the cascade's actual rules, the event loop's actual model — and ends where the
    syllabus's security topic meets the browser: injection.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the page pipeline — from file to pixels" widget="equationStep" config={webCfg} />

  <h2>HTML: structure with meaning</h2>
  <p>
    HTML is not layout; it is <strong>meaning</strong>. Elements declare what content <em>is</em> —
    <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> a heading, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> site navigation, <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>
    an action, <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> genuinely tabular data. The browser parses the text into the
    <strong>DOM</strong> (a live tree of nodes that JavaScript will manipulate), and the quality of that
    tree decides far more than looks: a screen reader announces the document from its landmarks; a search
    engine ranks it from its headings and semantics; a browser on a cheap phone renders it from structure
    alone. This is <strong>semantic HTML</strong>, and the professional habit is refusing "div soup" —
    nesting everything in anonymous boxes when <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>,
    <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> would say what each region is. The accessibility discipline (WCAG) rides the
    same choice: alt text on images, labels on inputs, keyboard-reachable controls, sufficient contrast.
    Note the asymmetry that makes accessibility cheap: done at the semantic level it is nearly free; done
    after the fact it is rework.
  </p>

  <h2>CSS: the cascade, specificity, and the box model</h2>
  <p>
    CSS styles the DOM with rules: a <strong>selector</strong> matches elements, declarations style them.
    Two mechanics carry most of the confusion, so learn them exactly. <strong>The cascade</strong> resolves
    competing rules: later rules win ties, but <strong>specificity</strong> outranks order — counted as
    (ids, classes, elements): <code>p</code> is (0,0,0,1); <code>.note</code> is (0,0,1,0) and beats any
    number of element selectors; <code>#header</code> (0,1,0,0) beats any class; inline styles beat all;
    <code>!important</code> breaks the arithmetic (which is why its overuse signals a codebase in trouble).
    <strong>The box model</strong>: every element is a rectangle of content wrapped in padding, border and
    margin — a width: 200px element with 20px padding and 2px border is 244px wide (unless
    <code>box-sizing: border-box</code>, the near-universal default, makes width include padding and
    border). Layout is the modern layer: <strong>flexbox</strong> for one-dimensional arrangement
    (toolbars, lists, centring) and <strong>grid</strong> for two-dimensional pages (named rows and
    columns); <strong>media queries</strong> apply rules conditional on viewport size, which is how one
    page serves phone and desktop — responsive design replacing the separate-mobile-site era.
  </p>

  <h2>JavaScript: the event loop's mental model</h2>
  <p>
    JavaScript is the browser's native behaviour language, and its execution model explains almost all web
    coding idioms: the language runs <strong>single-threaded</strong> on an <strong>event loop</strong>.
    Your code runs in short turns triggered by events (clicks, timers, network completions); blocking work
    — fetching data, waiting — is delegated to the browser, and your <strong>callback</strong> (today,
    usually a <code>Promise</code> awaited in <code>async</code> style) is scheduled when the result
    arrives. This is why a synchronous loop that runs for three seconds freezes the whole page (the loop
    cannot turn) while a three-second <code>fetch</code> does not (nothing is blocked). The working
    pattern: <code>fetch(url)</code> for data, then update the DOM — changing a node's text or class makes
    the browser recompute the affected layout and repaint. <strong>Progressive enhancement</strong> is the
    resilience rule: HTML that works alone, CSS that improves it, JS that upgrades it further — if
    JavaScript fails, the content still exists, a big reason the web outlived flashier rivals (Flash died
    at the end of 2020). Frameworks (React, Vue) automate the DOM-update loop with component state, but
    the pipeline underneath is unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline, and where the milliseconds go</h2>
  <p>
    The browser assembles a page in a fixed order worth memorising: parse HTML → build the DOM; parse CSS
    → build the CSSOM; combine into a <strong>render tree</strong>; <strong>layout</strong> (compute every
    box's geometry); <strong>paint</strong>; composite to the screen. JavaScript can interrupt anywhere,
    and DOM changes that alter geometry force re-layout — which is why performance work is mostly about
    the critical path: defer non-essential JS, size images (layout shifts from late-loading images are the
    "content jumps" you have felt), and keep the first render small. The economics are real: every
    kilobyte costs someone's data plan and a measurable fraction of users; caching, lazy loading and
    static generation (this atlas's approach) are how the fastest sites stay fast.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the browser is a simple document viewer." It is closer to an operating
  system: a JIT-compiled language runtime, a rendering engine, a network stack, storage, cryptography —
  sandboxed so that a random website can execute code on your machine safely. Which brings the lesson to
  the syllabus's security topic, because the browser's security model is learned through its classic
  failure.</p>

  <h2>Security meets the browser: injection</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>XSS (cross-site scripting)</strong> is the web's canonical vulnerability: if user-supplied
    text is inserted into a page without escaping, the browser cannot know the text was <em>data</em> —
    a comment reading <code>&lt;script&gt;steal()&lt;/script&gt;</code> executes in every visitor's
    browser, stealing sessions or rewriting the page. The defence is structural: escape at the boundary
    (render <code>&lt;</code> as <code>&amp;lt;</code>), use frameworks' auto-escaping, and apply a
    Content Security Policy that restricts where scripts may come from. The same boundary-discipline
    lesson appears as SQL injection at the database layer — a category, not an incident: <strong>whenever
    data and code share a channel, the parser's assumptions are the attack surface</strong>. Pair it with
    the browser's second core rule, the <em>same-origin policy</em> (one site's script cannot read
    another's data), plus HTTPS everywhere (the networking lesson's TLS), and you have the web's
    three-pillar security model: origin isolation, encrypted transport, escaped boundaries.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked build: the semantic skeleton of a page</h2>
  <p>
    Tie the trio together on a real micro-build — a recipe page — and watch each layer do exactly its
    job. <strong>HTML</strong> declares meaning only:
  </p>
  <p>
    &lt;main&gt; &lt;h1&gt;Flatbread&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;p&gt;Twenty minutes, four ingredients.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h2&gt;Ingredients&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;300 g flour&lt;/li&gt; ... &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;h2&gt;Method&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;ol&gt; &lt;li&gt;Mix and knead.&lt;/li&gt; ... &lt;/ol&gt;
    &lt;img src="flatbread.jpg" alt="Stack of flatbreads, charred in places"&gt; &lt;/main&gt;
  </p>
  <p>
    Note the choices that are accessibility, not decoration: one h1 (the page's title), headings in
    order (the screen-reader user navigates by heading level, exactly like skimming), a list element
    for an actual list (so each ingredient is its own announceable item), and an alt text that
    <em>describes rather than labels</em> ("flatbread.jpg" as alt text tells a blind user nothing).
    <strong>CSS</strong> then styles the semantics without touching them (a media query switches the
    ingredient list to two columns above 600 px width); <strong>JavaScript</strong> layers behaviour on
    top — a "scale recipe" control multiplies quantities, rewriting only the list-item text nodes, and
    crucially the page remains fully usable with JavaScript disabled. That is progressive enhancement
    as a concrete artefact: three layers, each removable, the content surviving every removal. Now you
    can also read the failure modes precisely: a div-soup version of the same page is not "ugly
    HTML" — it is a page whose headings, list structure and labels a screen reader and a search engine
    cannot reconstruct.
  </p>

  <h2>State, storage, and the request/response contract</h2>
  <p>
    Because HTTP is stateless, the browser-plus-server must reassemble continuity every time, and the
    places state lives are a design decision with security consequences. <strong>Cookies</strong>:
    server-issued key–value pairs the browser replays on every request to that site — the mechanism
    behind login sessions; flags matter (HttpOnly keeps cookies away from JavaScript — a direct XSS
    mitigation; Secure restricts them to HTTPS; SameSite curbs cross-site request forgery by not sending
    them on third-party requests). <strong>Web storage</strong> (localStorage, sessionStorage): larger
    per-origin key–value stores usable by scripts — convenient for preferences, wrong for session
    tokens (readable by any injected script). <strong>The request itself</strong>: verbs carry semantics
    (GET fetches without side effects, POST submits, PUT/DELETE fully replace or remove — the semantic
    difference is what makes caches, retries and proxies safe or dangerous), and status codes close the
    loop. The professional rule of thumb: identity state in HttpOnly cookies, UI state in the client,
    durable state in the database — anything else is state you have misplaced and will later find in a
    bug report. Together with the same-origin policy and escaped boundaries, this is the browser's whole
    security architecture: small rules, strictly enforced, doing enormous work.
  </p>

  <h2>JavaScript's object model, enough to be dangerous</h2>
  <p>
    Two features of the language explain most code you will read. <strong>Everything is a dynamic
    object</strong>: an object is a bag of named properties you can add, remove and inspect at run time
    (<code>user.role = "admin"</code> just works), and functions are values — assignable, passable,
    returnable — which is why callbacks, event handlers and framework components are all functions in
    transit. <strong>Closures</strong>: a function remembers the variables of the scope where it was
    defined, so a counter's state can live inside a function (<code>let count = 0; return () =>
    ++count</code>) with no global and no class — the mechanism behind every "private state" pattern in
    browser code, and the reason event handlers can reference the data of the loop iteration that
    created them. Add <code>this</code> (the object a method is called on — famously subtle), array
    methods that take functions (<code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>reduce</code> — the data
    transformation style the whole ecosystem uses), and JSON (objects as text, the web's lingua franca
    for payloads), and you hold the working core: the DOM API tells the objects what to change, closures
    hold the state between events, and the event loop decides when.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Rendering internals</strong> reward study: the compositor splits painting into layers so that
    scrolling and transforms stay on the GPU (which is why <code>transform</code> animations are cheap and
    <code>top/left</code> animations are expensive — one moves on the compositor, the other re-lays-out);
    the HTML parser is deliberately forgiving (the web must render anything ever published), which is why
    validators exist and why browsers never "crash on bad HTML." <strong>The platform keeps expanding</strong>:
    WebAssembly runs compiled C/Rust/Python at near-native speed in the same sandbox; service workers
    make pages work offline; WebGPU exposes the graphics card. Each addition re-raises the founding
    question of web architecture — <strong>progressive enhancement vs app-style single-page builds</strong> —
    a genuine engineering trade-off (resilience and reach against app-like interactivity). Open questions:
    can the accessibility layer keep pace as interfaces grow generative; how will the platform's
    economics — browser engines, ad-funded standards — evolve; and what does "the web" mean when most
    consumption is inside apps that merely borrow its protocols? You now hold the vocabulary to read those
    arguments in their native habitat: view source.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MDN Web Docs; Berners-Lee, "Information Management: A Proposal" (1989); W3C accessibility guidelines (WCAG); Zakas, <em>Professional JavaScript for Web Developers</em>.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: specificity, injection and the event loop" widget="quiz" config={webQuiz} />
</TopicPage>
